INT. PUPPET THEATRE - STAGE - NIGHT

Velvet curtains drawn back. Painted backdrops of a village. A hundred marionettes hang on rails above the stage like a gallows crowd. Dust motes in the work lights.

DETECTIVE PRUDENCE ACHEBE (40s, long coat, wry mouth) walks the boards, boots echoing. VASILI (70s, the puppeteer, hands knotted by arthritis) sits in a high chair at the lip of the stage. CELESTE (30s, costume designer, thread around her wrist) waits in the front row. JUDD (50s, theatre manager, blazer, sweat on his lip) hovers near the exit. OLD BEATRIX (80s, ticket-taker, cardigan, pearl necklace) knits in the aisle seat.

A marionette with a painted gentleman's face hangs center stage, its strings tied in a noose around a hook. Beneath it, on the boards, a chalk outline of a body.

ACHEBE
Everyone comfortable? Good. I'll be brief.

VASILI
That's not usual for the police.

ACHEBE
I'm told it's not usual for a puppeteer to be found dead beneath his own puppet either.

Celeste flinches. Beatrix's needles never stop.

ACHEBE (CONT'D)
Ms. Celeste, you sewed the costume.

CELESTE
Yes.

ACHEBE
Whose idea was the red thread in the collar?

CELESTE
It was in the sketches.

ACHEBE
Whose sketches?

CELESTE
Judd's.

Judd's jaw tightens. He shifts toward the exit. Achebe doesn't look at him.

ACHEBE
Mr. Judd. Sit down, please.

JUDD
I'm fine here.

ACHEBE
You're not. Beatrix, would you tell him where to sit?

BEATRIX
(not looking up)
Judd, dear. Do as the lady says.

Judd sits. Slowly. Vasili's fingers drum the arm of his chair.

ACHEBE
Mr. Vasili, you've been quiet.

VASILI
I'm old. Quiet is my right.

ACHEBE
You've been here forty years. The dead man was your partner.

VASILI
Thirty-eight. And he was not my partner. He was my rival.

ACHEBE
Rival for what?

VASILI
For the audience. For the last good company in this city.

ACHEBE
And now he's dead, and the company is yours.

VASILI
(softly)
Yes.

Achebe nods. She walks to the marionette, lifts its limp wooden hand, turns it over. A tiny brass key is wedged between the fingers.

ACHEBE
The key to the box office safe.

Judd's breath hitches.

ACHEBE (CONT'D)
Mr. Judd, you told my sergeant the safe was untouched.

JUDD
It was.

ACHEBE
Then why does the puppet have the key?

JUDD
(too quickly)
Because Vasili put it there.

VASILI
I would never.

JUDD
He's lying!

ACHEBE
Beatrix. Who handed the dead man his last cup of tea?

The needles stop. For the first time Beatrix looks up. Her eyes, clouded, rest on Achebe.

BEATRIX
I did, dear.

CELESTE
Oh, Beatrix.

BEATRIX
Don't fuss. He'd been stealing from the till for years. Poor Judd took the blame, and he's such a nervous boy. I just wanted it to stop.

Judd stares at her, mouth open. Achebe sets the marionette's hand down.

ACHEBE
Judd.

JUDD
Yes?

ACHEBE
You're free to go.

Judd bolts. The door slams. Celeste pulls the thread from her wrist and stares at it. Vasili lowers his head.

ACHEBE (CONT'D)
Mrs. Beatrix. I'll need you to come with me.

BEATRIX
Of course, dear. Let me finish the row.

She knits three more stitches. Then she folds the work into her lap, stands, and offers Achebe her arm.

ACHEBE
Thank you.

BEATRIX
Don't thank me. Take care of the little ones.

She nods at the hanging marionettes. As the two women walk up the aisle, the work lights dim one by one. The puppets sway on their strings.
